
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Recorded: server-side audit, no client tracking module. That removes the second subsystem entirely.

  One last requirements question, and it's the one where your word "persist" actually bites. Because the cookie is
  httpOnly, [1mJavaScript cannot read it[0m — so after a page reload, the client knows it's authenticated (the browser will
  send the cookie) but has no idea [3mwho[0m it is. The display copy has to come from somewhere.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the client re-learns the [38;5;153muserId[39m on a fresh page load. This sets what every future page's startup looks
  like, so it's worth settling before I write the module.

  - [1m[38;5;153m/me[39m call on page load[0m — the page asks the server who it is. The server is the single source of truth, so the UI can
    never claim a stale identity. Costs one round trip per page load and a brief "loading" state that every future form
    has to handle.
  - [1mMirror the [38;5;153muserId[39m in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — instant, no round trip, survives restart. Costs correctness: the cookie and the
    mirror expire independently, so when the session lapses the page still cheerfully shows "logged in as alice" until
    something fails. That desync is the classic bug here, and it's silent.
  - [1mDon't persist the display copy[0m — [38;5;153muserId[39m is known only in the moment right after login; other pages show nothing
    until the user acts. Cheapest by far, but it doesn't deliver "works across the app," which is the thing you added.

  [1mMy recommendation: the first.[0m It's the only option where the displayed identity and the real session can't diverge,
  and the round trip is the price of that guarantee. If the loading flash turns out to matter, the fix is caching the
  [38;5;153m/me[39m result for the page's lifetime — which is a later optimization, not a different architecture.
